\section[Overview]{Overview of Machine Learning Systems}

% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{When to Use Machine Learning}

\begin{frame}{What ML solutions generally do}
  \begin{withmargin}
    \begin{tdmain}
      \begin{tddef}{Machine Learning}
        An approach to \textbf{(1) learn} \textbf{(2) complex patterns} from
        \textbf{(3) existing data} and use these patterns to make
        \textbf{(4) predictions} on \textbf{(5) unseen data}.
      \end{tddef}
    \end{tdmain}
    \begin{tdmargin}
      ML is not a magic tool that can solve all problems.
      \par\medskip
      Even for problems that ML can solve, ML solutions might not be the
      optimal solutions.
    \end{tdmargin}
  \end{withmargin}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{(1) Learn}
  \alertbf{The system has the capacity to learn.}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item A relational database isn't an ML system. It doesn't have the
      capacity to learn.
    \item There must be something for it to learn from. In most cases, data.
    \item Supervised learning: from example input and output pairs, learn how
      to generate outputs for arbitrary inputs.
  \end{itemize}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{(2) Complex Patterns}
  \alertbf{There are patterns to learn, and they are complex.}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Only useful when there are patterns. No pattern in a fair die.
    \item There are patterns in how stocks are priced. Billions invested.
    \item Simple pattern, no ML. Zip code to state: use a lookup table.
    \item Rental price against its characteristics: very challenging to
      manually specify. ML is a good solution.
    \item What is complex to machines is different from what is complex to
      humans.
  \end{itemize}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{(3) Existing Data}
  \alertbf{Data is available, or it's possible to collect data.}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Because ML learns from data, there must be data to learn from.
    \item Zero-shot: no data for the task at hand, but still trained on data
      for other tasks. It still requires data to learn.
    \item Continual learning: deploy without training, learn from incoming
      data in production. Risk is poor customer experience.
    \item Fake it til you make it: serve predictions made by humans, train on
      the generated data later.
  \end{itemize}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{(4) Predictions}
  \alertbf{It's a predictive problem.}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item ML models make predictions. They can only solve problems that
      require predictive answers.
    \item Especially appealing when you benefit from a large quantity of cheap
      but approximate predictions.
    \item Whatever the question: ``What would the answer to this question
      be?'' Future, present, or past.
    \item Compute-intensive problems reframe well. Approximate the outcome
      instead of computing it.
  \end{itemize}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{(5) Unseen Data}
  \begin{withmargin}
    \begin{tdmain}
      \alertbf{Unseen data shares patterns with the training data.}
      \begin{itemize}
        \item Patterns learned are only useful if unseen data also share them.
        \item Unseen and training data should come from similar distributions.
        \item We don't know the distribution. We make assumptions, and hope
          they hold.
      \end{itemize}
    \end{tdmain}
    \begin{tdmargin}
      Predicting app downloads on Christmas 2020, trained on data from 2008,
      when the most popular app was Koi Pond.
      \par\medskip
      What's Koi Pond? Exactly.
    \end{tdmargin}
  \end{withmargin}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Catalysts}
  ML solutions will especially \alertbf{shine} with these additional
  characteristics.
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{It's repetitive.} Each pattern is repeated multiple times.
      Easier for machines to learn.
    \item \textbf{The cost of wrong predictions is cheap.} A bad
      recommendation is usually forgiving.
    \item \textbf{It's at scale.} Nontrivial up-front investment, so it makes
      sense to use the solution a lot.
    \item \textbf{The patterns are constantly changing.} Cultures change.
      Tastes change. Technologies change.
  \end{itemize}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{When Not to Use Machine Learning}
  Most of today's ML algorithms shouldn't be used under \alertbf{any} of the
  following conditions.
  \begin{itemize}
    \item It's unethical.
    \item Simpler solutions do the trick.
    \item It's not cost-effective.
  \end{itemize}
  \vskip0.5em
  Break the problem into smaller components, and use ML to solve some of them.
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Machine Learning Use Cases}
\end{frame}

